<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franco-Syrian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Franco-Syrian War was a short conflict in 1920 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the Arab Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> proclaimed under Faisal I. When Faisal's government rejected the French mandate awarded at San Remo, French forces advanced from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, defeated a small Syrian army at Maysalun on 24 July 1920 and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, ending the kingdom and beginning the French Mandate for Syria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franco-syrian-war.jpg" alt="Franco-Syrian War" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown army photopgrapher, Syria, died over 70 years ago · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maysaloun2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-allenby">Edmund Allenby</a>'s forces captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> on 30 September 1918, and Faisal, the Hashemite ruler, entered on 3 October during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-revolt">Arab Revolt</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. On 5 October, with Allenby's permission, he announced an Arab constitutional government. The secret Sykes-Picot Agreement of 1916 divided the Ottoman remnants between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-gouraud">Henri Gouraud</a> became French representative and commander of the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-national-congress">Syrian Congress</a> held its first session on 3 June 1919, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hashim-al-atassi">Hashim al-Atassi</a> as president. On 2 July it resolved for a fully independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> under Faisal and refused French claims. The Anglo-French Agreement of 15 September 1919 withdrew British troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from 1 November, leaving the French to answer only to themselves. In January 1920 Faisal negotiated with Clemenceau, but his anti-French supporters pressed him to reverse the compromise, and he did.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Attacks on French forces occurred across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from January 1920. On 8 March 1920 an independent Arab Kingdom of Syria was proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, with Faisal as king and al-Atassi as prime minister; the British and French repudiated it, and the San Remo Conference of April 1920 established the French mandate. Syrian nationalists rejected it. Gouraud issued an ultimatum to disband the Syrian troops, and Faisal surrendered on 14 July, but his defence minister Yusuf al-'Azma, ignoring him, led a small force of disbanded troops and civilians to Maysalun.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maysalun">Battle of Maysalun</a> on 24 July General Mariano Goybet's French forces easily defeated the Syrian forces, and al-'Azma was killed. French forces entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> the same day without resistance, and the next day the kingdom was abolished.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 25 July a new pro-French government was declared under 'Alaa al-Din al-Darubi. Gouraud established civil administration and divided the mandate into six states: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, the Alawite State, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-druze">Jabal Druze</a>, the autonomous Sanjak of Alexandretta, now Hatay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and the State of Greater Lebanon, which became modern Lebanon. The British, concerned for their position in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, agreed to make the fugitive Faisal king of Iraq.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Franco-Syrian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
